\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Discussion}
\title{Products and derivatives}
\date{2026-11-04}


\p{A coupling that varies from site to site enters a lattice action as a
coefficient: the term #{m\sum_v\varphi(v)^2} for a constant mass #{m} becomes
#{\sum_v m(v)\varphi(v)^2}, so a fixed field multiplies the field at each site.
Multiplication by a fixed vertex field acts on vertex fields, while the
difference goes from vertex fields to edge fields, so comparing the two orders
needs the multiplier carried to the edges by an endpoint pullback. That
comparison is the endpoint product identity of \ref{fdip-0012}. What is the map
that multiplies, and how badly does it fail to commute with a difference?}
